//? Title: Bayesian Linear Regression and Model Averaging
//? Subtitle: Exact Gaussian posteriors for nested polynomial models
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

\abstract(
Fit three polynomial regressions to the same noisy observations. Their exact
marginal likelihoods update equal prior model probabilities. Averaging the
posterior predictions accounts for uncertainty in both the coefficients and
the polynomial degree.
)
\toc

> Model and evidence

For design matrix $X$, the coefficients have a zero-mean Gaussian prior with
covariance $\tau^2 I$. Observations are conditionally independent with known
noise variance $\sigma^2$. The model includes the intercept in the prior.
Let $P$ denote posterior precision. Cholesky solves give the conditional
coefficient mean and covariance.

$$
P=\tau^{-2}I+\sigma^{-2}X^\top X,\qquad
m=P^{-1}X^\top y/\sigma^2,\qquad
\operatorname{Cov}(w\mid y)=P^{-1}.
$$
\alttext(Posterior precision combines the isotropic prior and the design cross-product. Solving against the precision yields the coefficient mean and covariance.)

At a query row $q$, the fitted mean is $qm$ and the variance for a new
response is $\sigma^2+qP^{-1}q^\top$. The log marginal likelihood integrates
over the coefficients. This integral depends on both the fit and the prior,
so it gives a different criterion from residual error at fitted coefficients.

```{cpp, file="figures/bayesian_linear_regression_concept.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram;
  diagram.node("data", {0, 0}, "32 noisy observations\nthree polynomial bases");
  diagram.node("fit", {5.2, 0}, "Gaussian conditioning\nposterior and evidence");
  diagram.node("predict", {10.4, 0}, "Weight by model evidence\naverage predictive moments");
  diagram.edge("data", "fit").edge("fit", "predict");
  diagram.save_svg("build/bayesian_linear_regression_concept.svg");
}
```
